    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Slack better than Microsoft Teams?</h3>
    <p>Slack is better for fast, channel-based communication and third-party integrations. Teams is better for organizations that already use Microsoft 365 because it bundles chat, video, file storage, and Office apps. For startups and cross-company collaboration, Slack wins. For Microsoft-centric enterprises, Teams wins.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Slack or Teams?</h3>
    <p>Teams is cheaper when bundled with Microsoft 365. Microsoft 365 Business Basic at $6 per user per month includes Teams, Exchange, OneDrive, and web Office apps. Slack Pro at $7.25 per user per month only includes Slack. For teams that need Office apps anyway, Teams is effectively free. For teams that only need chat, Slack Pro is comparable in price.</p>

    <h3>Can Slack and Teams work together?</h3>
    <p>Slack and Teams do not integrate natively, but third-party tools like Mio and ChatApp bridge messages between platforms. Most organizations pick one platform to avoid fragmentation. A common pattern is Teams for internal communication and Slack Connect for cross-company channels with external partners.</p>

    <h3>Which has better video conferencing?</h3>
    <p>Teams has better built-in video conferencing. Teams supports up to 1,000 participants in interactive meetings and 10,000 in view-only mode, with built-in recording, transcription, and Microsoft AI features. Slack's built-in video tool, Huddles, supports up to 50 participants with screen sharing. Slack integrates with Zoom for larger meetings, but Zoom requires a separate subscription.</p>

    <h3>Does Slack have more integrations than Teams?</h3>
    <p>Yes, Slack has over 2,400 third-party app integrations compared to Teams' approximately 1,800. Slack's app directory is broader, especially for developer tools, marketing platforms, and modern SaaS apps. Teams has stronger integration with Microsoft products like SharePoint, OneNote, and Power BI, plus the broader Microsoft Graph API.</p>

    <h3>Which is more secure?</h3>
    <p>Both meet enterprise security standards with SSO, SCIM provisioning, audit logs, and data loss prevention. Teams has a slight edge for regulated industries because of Microsoft Purview compliance, eDiscovery, and native integration with Active Directory. Slack matches most enterprise requirements but third-party tools fill gaps in archiving and compliance.</p>

